{"id":5186,"date":"2018-12-07T12:59:47","date_gmt":"2018-12-07T17:59:47","guid":{"rendered":"http:\/\/iboishin.github.io/portfolio-website\/?p=5186"},"modified":"2020-03-08T14:20:14","modified_gmt":"2020-03-08T18:20:14","slug":"maastricht-the-netherlands","status":"publish","type":"post","link":"https:\/\/iboishin.github.io/portfolio-website\/travel-log\/maastricht-the-netherlands\/","title":{"rendered":"Maastricht, The Netherlands"},"content":{"rendered":"<p>Maastricht is a quaint university town just like Lille. With the gorgeous architecture  and the peaceful river, Maastricht should be on everyone&#8217;s travel list!<\/p>\n<p>A special thank you to the <a href=\"https:\/\/www.visitmaastricht.com\/things-to-do\/guided-tours\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><u>Maastricht Tourism Office<\/u><\/a> for all of the information.<\/p>\n<h5>P.S. If you go during the winter and are looking for a nice warm snack to have while you people-watch, the restaurant outside of the church-turned-library has the most delicious bitterballen and very comfortable winter outside seating.<\/h5>\n<div class=\"su-tabs su-tabs-style-default su-tabs-mobile-stack\" data-active=\"1\" data-scroll-offset=\"0\" data-anchor-in-url=\"no\"><div class=\"su-tabs-nav\"><span class=\"\" data-url=\"\" data-target=\"blank\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"button\">City Walk<\/span><span class=\"\" data-url=\"\" data-target=\"blank\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"button\">St. Pieter Fort<\/span><span class=\"\" data-url=\"\" data-target=\"blank\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"button\">North Caves<\/span><\/div><div class=\"su-tabs-panes\"><div class=\"su-tabs-pane su-u-clearfix su-u-trim\" data-title=\"City Walk\">\n<style type=\"text\/css\">\r\n\t#foogallery-gallery-3848 .fg-image {\r\n        width: 150px;\r\n    }\r\n<\/style>\r\n\t\t\t\t<div class=\"foogallery foogallery-container foogallery-default foogallery-lightbox-foobox fg-gutter-10 fg-center fg-default fg-light fg-border-thin fg-shadow-outline fg-loading-default fg-loaded-fade-in fg-caption-hover fg-hover-fade fg-hover-zoom fg-ready\" id=\"foogallery-gallery-3848\" data-foogallery=\"{&quot;item&quot;:{&quot;showCaptionTitle&quot;:true,&quot;showCaptionDescription&quot;:true},&quot;lazy&quot;:true}\" >\r\n\t<div class=\"fg-item fg-type-image fg-idle\"><figure class=\"fg-item-inner\"><a href=\"https:\/\/iboishin.github.io/portfolio-website\/wp-content\/uploads\/2019\/01\/DSC_4582.jpg\" data-caption-title=\"The Romans built the first bridge in Maastricht in the 13th century. Made of wood, the bridge collapsed in 1275 and was rebuilt in stone. The Sint-Servaasbrug bridge is believed to be the oldest bridge in the Netherlands.\" data-attachment-id=\"3852\" class=\"fg-thumb\"><span class=\"fg-image-wrap\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" title=\"The Romans built the first bridge in Maastricht in the 13th century. Made of wood, the bridge collapsed in 1275 and was rebuilt in stone. The Sint-Servaasbrug bridge is believed to be the oldest bridge in the Netherlands.\" width=\"150\" height=\"150\" class=\"skip-lazy fg-image\" data-src-fg=\"https:\/\/iboishin.github.io/portfolio-website\/wp-content\/uploads\/cache\/2019\/01\/DSC_4582\/1055152527.jpg\" src=\"data:image\/svg+xml,%3Csvg%20xmlns%3D%22http%3A%2F%2Fwww.w3.org%2F2000%2Fsvg%22%20width%3D%22150%22%20height%3D%22150%22%20viewBox%3D%220%200%20150%20150%22%3E%3C%2Fsvg%3E\"><\/span><span class=\"fg-image-overlay\"><\/span><\/a><figcaption class=\"fg-caption\"><div class=\"fg-caption-inner\"><div class=\"fg-caption-title\">The Romans built the first bridge in Maastricht in the 13th century. Made of wood, the bridge collapsed in 1275 and was rebuilt in stone. The Sint-Servaasbrug bridge is believed to be the oldest bridge in the Netherlands.<\/div><\/div><\/figcaption><\/figure><div class=\"fg-loader\"><\/div><\/div><div class=\"fg-item fg-type-image fg-idle\"><figure class=\"fg-item-inner\"><a href=\"https:\/\/iboishin.github.io/portfolio-website\/wp-content\/uploads\/2019\/01\/DSC_4585.jpg\" data-caption-title=\"With its brick, limestone and grey-stone facade, this building is a typical example of the Maastricht renaissance. At a time when people could not yet read, pictograms were used to define the building&#039;s purpose. Can you guess what this building was used for originally? [I won&#039;t spoil your fun ;) The answer is in the next description.]\" data-attachment-id=\"3853\" class=\"fg-thumb\"><span class=\"fg-image-wrap\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" title=\"With its brick, limestone and grey-stone facade, this building is a typical example of the Maastricht renaissance. At a time when people could not yet read, pictograms were used to define the building&#039;s purpose. Can you guess what this building was used for originally? [I won&#039;t spoil your fun ;) The answer is in the next description.]\" width=\"150\" height=\"150\" class=\"skip-lazy fg-image\" data-src-fg=\"https:\/\/iboishin.github.io/portfolio-website\/wp-content\/uploads\/cache\/2019\/01\/DSC_4585\/2449095390.jpg\" src=\"data:image\/svg+xml,%3Csvg%20xmlns%3D%22http%3A%2F%2Fwww.w3.org%2F2000%2Fsvg%22%20width%3D%22150%22%20height%3D%22150%22%20viewBox%3D%220%200%20150%20150%22%3E%3C%2Fsvg%3E\"><\/span><span class=\"fg-image-overlay\"><\/span><\/a><figcaption class=\"fg-caption\"><div class=\"fg-caption-inner\"><div class=\"fg-caption-title\">With its brick, limestone and grey-stone facade, this building is a typical example of the Maastricht renaissance. At a time when people could not yet read, pictograms were used to define the building's purpose. Can you guess what this building was used for originally? [I won't spoil your fun \ud83d\ude09 The answer is in the next description.]<\/div><\/div><\/figcaption><\/figure><div class=\"fg-loader\"><\/div><\/div><div class=\"fg-item fg-type-image fg-idle\"><figure class=\"fg-item-inner\"><a href=\"https:\/\/iboishin.github.io/portfolio-website\/wp-content\/uploads\/2019\/01\/DSC_4586.jpg\" data-caption-title=\"&lt;p&gt;There are several instances around town that suggest the strong influence that the French had on the city. In French architecture, the use of stone is used as a symbol of wealth as it was expensive to buy and transport. While these owners wanted to show that they were wealthy, notice that they have only done the facade in stone. The rest of the house is actually built out of brick.\n&lt;p&gt;(Answer : Notice the gold lion on the facade of the previous picture. In French, &quot;gold lion&quot; is &lt;i&gt;lion d&#039;or&lt;\/i&gt;, which sounds remarkably similar to &lt;i&gt;lit on dort&lt;\/i&gt; meaning &quot;bed on which we sleep&quot;. Thus, the building on the previous picture was likely a hotel.)\" data-attachment-id=\"3854\" class=\"fg-thumb\"><span class=\"fg-image-wrap\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" title=\"&lt;p&gt;There are several instances around town that suggest the strong influence that the French had on the city. In French architecture, the use of stone is used as a symbol of wealth as it was expensive to buy and transport. While these owners wanted to show that they were wealthy, notice that they have only done the facade in stone. The rest of the house is actually built out of brick.\n&lt;p&gt;(Answer : Notice the gold lion on the facade of the previous picture. In French, &quot;gold lion&quot; is &lt;i&gt;lion d&#039;or&lt;\/i&gt;, which sounds remarkably similar to &lt;i&gt;lit on dort&lt;\/i&gt; meaning &quot;bed on which we sleep&quot;. Thus, the building on the previous picture was likely a hotel.)\" width=\"150\" height=\"150\" class=\"skip-lazy fg-image\" data-src-fg=\"https:\/\/iboishin.github.io/portfolio-website\/wp-content\/uploads\/cache\/2019\/01\/DSC_4586\/3499566663.jpg\" src=\"data:image\/svg+xml,%3Csvg%20xmlns%3D%22http%3A%2F%2Fwww.w3.org%2F2000%2Fsvg%22%20width%3D%22150%22%20height%3D%22150%22%20viewBox%3D%220%200%20150%20150%22%3E%3C%2Fsvg%3E\"><\/span><span class=\"fg-image-overlay\"><\/span><\/a><figcaption class=\"fg-caption\"><div class=\"fg-caption-inner\"><div class=\"fg-caption-title\"><p>There are several instances around town that suggest the strong influence that the French had on the city. In French architecture, the use of stone is used as a symbol of wealth as it was expensive to buy and transport. While these owners wanted to show that they were wealthy, notice that they have only done the facade in stone. The rest of the house is actually built out of brick.\n<p>(Answer : Notice the gold lion on the facade of the previous picture. In French, \"gold lion\" is <i>lion d'or<\/i>, which sounds remarkably similar to <i>lit on dort<\/i> meaning \"bed on which we sleep\". Thus, the building on the previous picture was likely a hotel.)<\/div><\/div><\/figcaption><\/figure><div class=\"fg-loader\"><\/div><\/div><div class=\"fg-item fg-type-image fg-idle\"><figure class=\"fg-item-inner\"><a href=\"https:\/\/iboishin.github.io/portfolio-website\/wp-content\/uploads\/2019\/01\/DSC_4588.jpg\" data-caption-title=\"Following that logic, one might think that the owners of this house were significantly wealthier. Yet, if you look closely, these owners also took a shortcut. They simply took a less obvious one. Do you see a difference between the stone on the facade and that one the side? The stone on the facade is grey-stone, while that on the side is limestone, which was produced nearby and significantly cheaper to acquire.\" data-attachment-id=\"3855\" class=\"fg-thumb\"><span class=\"fg-image-wrap\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" title=\"Following that logic, one might think that the owners of this house were significantly wealthier. Yet, if you look closely, these owners also took a shortcut. They simply took a less obvious one. Do you see a difference between the stone on the facade and that one the side? The stone on the facade is grey-stone, while that on the side is limestone, which was produced nearby and significantly cheaper to acquire.\" width=\"150\" height=\"150\" class=\"skip-lazy fg-image\" data-src-fg=\"https:\/\/iboishin.github.io/portfolio-website\/wp-content\/uploads\/cache\/2019\/01\/DSC_4588\/1623612989.jpg\" src=\"data:image\/svg+xml,%3Csvg%20xmlns%3D%22http%3A%2F%2Fwww.w3.org%2F2000%2Fsvg%22%20width%3D%22150%22%20height%3D%22150%22%20viewBox%3D%220%200%20150%20150%22%3E%3C%2Fsvg%3E\"><\/span><span class=\"fg-image-overlay\"><\/span><\/a><figcaption class=\"fg-caption\"><div class=\"fg-caption-inner\"><div class=\"fg-caption-title\">Following that logic, one might think that the owners of this house were significantly wealthier. Yet, if you look closely, these owners also took a shortcut. They simply took a less obvious one. Do you see a difference between the stone on the facade and that one the side? The stone on the facade is grey-stone, while that on the side is limestone, which was produced nearby and significantly cheaper to acquire.<\/div><\/div><\/figcaption><\/figure><div class=\"fg-loader\"><\/div><\/div><div class=\"fg-item fg-type-image fg-idle\"><figure class=\"fg-item-inner\"><a href=\"https:\/\/iboishin.github.io/portfolio-website\/wp-content\/uploads\/2019\/01\/DSC_4593.jpg\" data-caption-title=\"The square Plein 1992 is at the heart of the old ceramic district. In the 1830s, the Netherlands built its first steam engine. After that, the technology was used to power the Industrial Revolution in the Netherlands. The ceramic industry was at the heart of this Industrial Revolution. The white greenhouse-looking building was built in 1880 to serve as a factory for Soci\u00e9t\u00e9 C\u00e9ramique. (Today, it is a theater.) The tall building to the right is called the Centre C\u00e9ramique, a museum explaining the history of the ceramic industry in Maastricht. In 2006, the two largest ceramics companies--Spinx and Soci\u00e9t\u00e9 C\u00e9ramique merged--giving way to the revitalization of the district. &lt;p&gt;Note: If you are there in person, take a look at the ground. It will give you a hint about the importance of the building at the next stop.&lt;\/p&gt;\" data-attachment-id=\"3856\" class=\"fg-thumb\"><span class=\"fg-image-wrap\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" title=\"The square Plein 1992 is at the heart of the old ceramic district. In the 1830s, the Netherlands built its first steam engine. After that, the technology was used to power the Industrial Revolution in the Netherlands. The ceramic industry was at the heart of this Industrial Revolution. The white greenhouse-looking building was built in 1880 to serve as a factory for Soci\u00e9t\u00e9 C\u00e9ramique. (Today, it is a theater.) The tall building to the right is called the Centre C\u00e9ramique, a museum explaining the history of the ceramic industry in Maastricht. In 2006, the two largest ceramics companies--Spinx and Soci\u00e9t\u00e9 C\u00e9ramique merged--giving way to the revitalization of the district. &lt;p&gt;Note: If you are there in person, take a look at the ground. It will give you a hint about the importance of the building at the next stop.&lt;\/p&gt;\" width=\"150\" height=\"150\" class=\"skip-lazy fg-image\" data-src-fg=\"https:\/\/iboishin.github.io/portfolio-website\/wp-content\/uploads\/cache\/2019\/01\/DSC_4593\/533458609.jpg\" src=\"data:image\/svg+xml,%3Csvg%20xmlns%3D%22http%3A%2F%2Fwww.w3.org%2F2000%2Fsvg%22%20width%3D%22150%22%20height%3D%22150%22%20viewBox%3D%220%200%20150%20150%22%3E%3C%2Fsvg%3E\"><\/span><span class=\"fg-image-overlay\"><\/span><\/a><figcaption class=\"fg-caption\"><div class=\"fg-caption-inner\"><div class=\"fg-caption-title\">The square Plein 1992 is at the heart of the old ceramic district. In the 1830s, the Netherlands built its first steam engine. After that, the technology was used to power the Industrial Revolution in the Netherlands. The ceramic industry was at the heart of this Industrial Revolution. The white greenhouse-looking building was built in 1880 to serve as a factory for Soci\u00e9t\u00e9 C\u00e9ramique. (Today, it is a theater.) The tall building to the right is called the Centre C\u00e9ramique, a museum explaining the history of the ceramic industry in Maastricht. In 2006, the two largest ceramics companies--Spinx and Soci\u00e9t\u00e9 C\u00e9ramique merged--giving way to the revitalization of the district. <p>Note: If you are there in person, take a look at the ground. It will give you a hint about the importance of the building at the next stop.<\/p><\/div><\/div><\/figcaption><\/figure><div class=\"fg-loader\"><\/div><\/div><div class=\"fg-item fg-type-image fg-idle\"><figure class=\"fg-item-inner\"><a href=\"https:\/\/iboishin.github.io/portfolio-website\/wp-content\/uploads\/2019\/01\/DSC_4595.jpg\" data-caption-title=\"The two fire-shaped buildings in the distance house the government of the Dutch province of Limburg, of which Maastricht is the capital. If you look closely, you will see that the two fire-shapes are actually connected by a bridge to form one large building. Built in 1986, this building housed the signing of the 1992 Treaty of Maastricht, which created the European Union and the Euro.\n(Here&#039;s a &lt;a href=&quot;https:\/\/www.architonic.com\/en\/project\/trilux-provincial-building-limburg\/5102113&quot;&gt;&lt;u&gt;link&lt;\/u&gt;&lt;\/a&gt; that do justice to the intricacy of the building.)\" data-attachment-id=\"3857\" class=\"fg-thumb\"><span class=\"fg-image-wrap\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" title=\"The two fire-shaped buildings in the distance house the government of the Dutch province of Limburg, of which Maastricht is the capital. If you look closely, you will see that the two fire-shapes are actually connected by a bridge to form one large building. Built in 1986, this building housed the signing of the 1992 Treaty of Maastricht, which created the European Union and the Euro.\n(Here&#039;s a &lt;a href=&quot;https:\/\/www.architonic.com\/en\/project\/trilux-provincial-building-limburg\/5102113&quot;&gt;&lt;u&gt;link&lt;\/u&gt;&lt;\/a&gt; that do justice to the intricacy of the building.)\" width=\"150\" height=\"150\" class=\"skip-lazy fg-image\" data-src-fg=\"https:\/\/iboishin.github.io/portfolio-website\/wp-content\/uploads\/cache\/2019\/01\/DSC_4595\/2088734078.jpg\" src=\"data:image\/svg+xml,%3Csvg%20xmlns%3D%22http%3A%2F%2Fwww.w3.org%2F2000%2Fsvg%22%20width%3D%22150%22%20height%3D%22150%22%20viewBox%3D%220%200%20150%20150%22%3E%3C%2Fsvg%3E\"><\/span><span class=\"fg-image-overlay\"><\/span><\/a><figcaption class=\"fg-caption\"><div class=\"fg-caption-inner\"><div class=\"fg-caption-title\">The two fire-shaped buildings in the distance house the government of the Dutch province of Limburg, of which Maastricht is the capital. If you look closely, you will see that the two fire-shapes are actually connected by a bridge to form one large building. Built in 1986, this building housed the signing of the 1992 Treaty of Maastricht, which created the European Union and the Euro.\n(Here's a <a href=\"https:\/\/www.architonic.com\/en\/project\/trilux-provincial-building-limburg\/5102113\"><u>link<\/u><\/a> that do justice to the intricacy of the building.)<\/div><\/div><\/figcaption><\/figure><div class=\"fg-loader\"><\/div><\/div><div class=\"fg-item fg-type-image fg-idle\"><figure class=\"fg-item-inner\"><a href=\"https:\/\/iboishin.github.io/portfolio-website\/wp-content\/uploads\/2019\/01\/DSC_4598.jpg\" data-caption-title=\"In Maastricht, you will notice throughout the city the remnants of the city fortification wall, which was in place until 1900.\" data-attachment-id=\"3858\" class=\"fg-thumb\"><span class=\"fg-image-wrap\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" title=\"In Maastricht, you will notice throughout the city the remnants of the city fortification wall, which was in place until 1900.\" width=\"150\" height=\"150\" class=\"skip-lazy fg-image\" data-src-fg=\"https:\/\/iboishin.github.io/portfolio-website\/wp-content\/uploads\/cache\/2019\/01\/DSC_4598\/102023762.jpg\" src=\"data:image\/svg+xml,%3Csvg%20xmlns%3D%22http%3A%2F%2Fwww.w3.org%2F2000%2Fsvg%22%20width%3D%22150%22%20height%3D%22150%22%20viewBox%3D%220%200%20150%20150%22%3E%3C%2Fsvg%3E\"><\/span><span class=\"fg-image-overlay\"><\/span><\/a><figcaption class=\"fg-caption\"><div class=\"fg-caption-inner\"><div class=\"fg-caption-title\">In Maastricht, you will notice throughout the city the remnants of the city fortification wall, which was in place until 1900.<\/div><\/div><\/figcaption><\/figure><div class=\"fg-loader\"><\/div><\/div><div class=\"fg-item fg-type-image fg-idle\"><figure class=\"fg-item-inner\"><a href=\"https:\/\/iboishin.github.io/portfolio-website\/wp-content\/uploads\/2019\/01\/20190102_143515.jpg\" data-caption-title=\"Helport is the last remaining gate in the city fortifications. Built in the late 13th century, it got its name from the adjacent street : Hell Street.\" data-attachment-id=\"3884\" class=\"fg-thumb\"><span class=\"fg-image-wrap\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" title=\"Helport is the last remaining gate in the city fortifications. Built in the late 13th century, it got its name from the adjacent street : Hell Street.\" width=\"150\" height=\"150\" class=\"skip-lazy fg-image\" data-src-fg=\"https:\/\/iboishin.github.io/portfolio-website\/wp-content\/uploads\/cache\/2019\/01\/20190102_143515\/1427700979.jpg\" src=\"data:image\/svg+xml,%3Csvg%20xmlns%3D%22http%3A%2F%2Fwww.w3.org%2F2000%2Fsvg%22%20width%3D%22150%22%20height%3D%22150%22%20viewBox%3D%220%200%20150%20150%22%3E%3C%2Fsvg%3E\"><\/span><span class=\"fg-image-overlay\"><\/span><\/a><figcaption class=\"fg-caption\"><div class=\"fg-caption-inner\"><div class=\"fg-caption-title\">Helport is the last remaining gate in the city fortifications. Built in the late 13th century, it got its name from the adjacent street : Hell Street.<\/div><\/div><\/figcaption><\/figure><div class=\"fg-loader\"><\/div><\/div><div class=\"fg-item fg-type-image fg-idle\"><figure class=\"fg-it